  const blogs = [
    {id:'blog-1',owner:'NailHub.AI',origin:'NailHub.AI',title:'Choosing a gel lamp: questions to ask before buying',topic:'Kỹ thuật Nail',summary:'Lessons from a salon with multiple stations: check power cords, removable bases, cleaning and testing before adding lamps.',body:'A salon with multiple stations does not need to choose the same lamp model simply because it is being promoted widely. Our first concern is whether the lamp suits our current products and station workflow.\n\nWhen viewing a lamp, I bring the products we use, ask for supplier instructions and check whether the power cord is easy to replace. A removable base makes cleaning easier; controls should also be clearly visible during continuous work.\n\nBefore buying several units, we try one during real appointments and note what technicians find helpful or awkward. A working backup helps prevent interruptions during busy shifts.',location:'Houston, TX',cover:images.workshop,publishedAt:at(2,10)},
    {id:'blog-2',owner:'BITCOIN NAIL BAR',origin:'NEXORA',title:'Refreshing the waiting area: start with the client walkway',topic:'Quản lý tiệm',summary:'Changing a row of chairs, moving a display and observing busy hours helps a salon decide which furniture to keep and which to sell.',body:'When we first refreshed our waiting area, we only thought about adding chairs. After a few busy weekends, we realized the biggest problem was the path from the door to the counter.\n\nWe observed peak hours and noted where clients stopped and where technicians needed to pass. We removed one row of chairs, moved the polish rack farther inside and placed information where seated clients could see it.\n\nWe photographed the actual condition of unused furniture, measured it and listed it with clear quantities. The waiting area now has fewer items but works better for clients and staff.',location:'Houston, TX',cover:images.salon,publishedAt:at(3,9)},
    {id:'blog-shift-handover',owner:'Linh Nguyễn',origin:'NEXORA',title:'A clear shift handover in 10 minutes',topic:'Quản lý tiệm',summary:'A short list of tools, supplies and client appointments saves the next shift from asking about every detail.',body:'Small salons often hand over shifts in a quick conversation. When the person leaving is rushed, details such as a polish color running low or a client changing services can be missed.\n\nOur team agreed on three sections: prepared stations and tools, supplies to restock and appointments with notes. Each section needs only a short, readable line; the incoming person reviews it and asks about anything unclear.\n\nAfter a week, we shortened the list together. We removed unused items and made frequently questioned items more specific. The goal is for the incoming person to know where to start.',location:'Dallas, TX',cover:images.reception,publishedAt:at(1,11)},
    {id:'blog-lunch-prep',owner:'Saigon Kitchen',origin:'NEXORA',title:'Preparing lunch service for more group bookings',topic:'Nhà hàng',summary:'Agreeing on table sections, prep times and table arrangements before opening helps the service team stay organized.',body:'For our restaurant, group bookings mean more than extra dishes to prepare. Table arrangements, order-taking responsibilities and serving times also need discussion ahead.\n\nAt the start of the day, the manager notes expected guest numbers, table assignments and tasks to finish before 11 AM. The kitchen and servers review the same information so they know when to report changes.\n\nAt the end of the shift, we spend a few minutes noting issues, such as a table that waited too long or supplies we had to search for. These small notes are more useful than simply remembering that the restaurant was busy.',location:'Houston, TX',cover:images.restaurant,publishedAt:at(4,10)},
    {id:'blog-photo-portfolio',owner:'Hà Nguyễn',origin:'NailHub.AI',title:'Photographing nail sets to help clients choose a style',topic:'Kỹ thuật Nail',summary:'Keep lighting and angles reasonably consistent and include length and service details alongside your photos.',body:'Clients often send a photo from our page and ask whether the style works on short nails. I started adding the service, length and differences between the reference image and the finished set.\n\nI photograph near a window with a simple background and include the whole hand rather than just one finger. Colors can still look different under salon lighting, so I remind clients to check physical swatches when they arrive.\n\nA small, consistent photo collection helps clients picture the result. I also ask permission before using photos of clients\' hands on the salon page.',location:'Houston, TX',cover:images.blush,publishedAt:at(5,13)},
    {id:'blog-may-first-appointment',owner:'Mây Beauty Studio',origin:'NEXORA',title:'First appointments: allow time to listen',topic:'Quản lý tiệm',summary:'Mây leaves extra time before a first service, confirms client concerns and agrees on the expected duration when booking.',body:'Some first-time clients bring a list of questions; others bring only a photo and are unsure which service to choose. Scheduling them as tightly as regular clients can leave both client and technician feeling rushed.\n\nOur studio started adding 15 minutes to first visits. The confirmation message states the arrival time, service under discussion and expected duration. Notes contain only what is needed to prepare; anything unclear is discussed in person.\n\nAt the end of the day, I review appointments that needed more time and ask technicians which details they often repeat. After a few weeks, we had fewer tightly packed appointments, and clients knew how long to allow for their visit.',location:'Dallas, TX',cover:images.spa,publishedAt:at(1,9,15)},
    {id:'blog-bright-stock-list',owner:'Bright Nail Supply',origin:'NEXORA',title:'A supply list that helps salons order without overbuying',topic:'Kinh doanh',summary:'Separate immediate needs from backups, record the right product codes and check stock before visiting the store.',body:'Our regular customers often visit after a long shift. Previously, some remembered a product name but forgot the size or code and had to call the salon to ask.\n\nWe suggest keeping a short list with the product name, code or packaging photo, remaining quantity and quantity to buy. Group next week\'s essentials together and clearly mark items needed only for specific appointments.\n\nWhen a list arrives, we check stock and confirm before preparing the order. At the counter, we review every item and quantity together. It takes a few extra minutes initially but reduces returns caused by choosing the wrong type.',location:'Houston, TX',cover:images.retail,publishedAt:at(3,10,20)},
    {id:'blog-bistro-morning-tables',owner:'Saigon Bistro',origin:'NEXORA',title:'Morning group bookings: arrange tables and confirm orders step by step',topic:'Nhà hàng',summary:'A message asking party size and arrival time helps prepare tables, cups and staff assignments before guests arrive.',body:'On weekend mornings, we welcome both couples and groups meeting for coffee. When a group arrives alongside smaller parties, the hardest things to arrange can be an order taker and space for dishes waiting to be served.\n\nWe ask ahead for party size, arrival time and whether children are coming. Before opening, the team marks tables to join, prepares enough cups and agrees who will serve the group. Orders are read back for confirmation before being sent to the counter.\n\nAfter the shift, we review which tables waited and which steps slowed us down. Sometimes moving clean cups closer to the counter makes the next shift easier. We keep small changes that the service team finds useful.',location:'Dallas, TX',cover:images.cafe,publishedAt:at(2,8,30)}
  ];

